 ],
 "literal": "Glow-going sky becoming-red, affliction having-intensified, / light-ceasing time, jasmine blooming / evening, they say, those who are confused indeed. / Crested rooster in-the-large-house sounding / great-dawning dawn also is evening, / daytime also is evening, to those who do not have a companion indeed.",
 "translation": "Those who say evening is only when the glowing sun sets, the sky turns red, grief intensifies, and the jasmine blooms as light fades, are surely confused. For those who are without their companion, even the early dawn when the crested rooster crows in the large house is evening, and the bright daytime is evening too.",
 "modern": "ஒளிரும் சூரியன் மறையும் வானம் சிவக்க, துன்பம் மிகுந்து, பகல் வெளிச்சம் அழியும் பொழுதில் முல்லை மலரும் மாலைப் பொழுதுதான் மாலைக் காலம் என்று கூறுபவர்கள் அறியாமையால் மயங்கியவர்கள் ஆவர். தங்களுக்குரிய துணை இல்லாதவர்களுக்கு, கொண்டையையுடைய சேவல் பெரிய வீட்டில் கூவுகின்ற விடியற்காலமும் மாலைப் பொழுதுதான்; பகல் பொழுதும் மாலைப் பொழுதுதான்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her companion, expressing her deep grief during separation from her lover in the mullai (pastoral) landscape. She challenges the conventional definition of evening, which people associate only with the setting sun and blooming jasmines. For her, the pain of separation is so relentless that every part of the day feels like the agonizing evening. Even the early dawn, marked by the crested rooster crowing in the large house, and the bright daytime feel like evening to those who are lonely. The images of the rooster and the empty house emphasize her solitude and the endless nature of her longing.",
